Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:12:23 +0300
Subject: vbo: index array min/max in AltiVec (NV30_MINMAX_VMX)

A draw from client vertex arrays needs the range of vertices its indices use, so Mesa scans the
index array of every such glDrawElements (vbo_get_minmax_index_mapped). SuperTuxKart's legacy
renderer (Irrlicht) draws its meshes from client arrays, and the scan was 6.5% of its CPU in the
profile of 2026-10-19 (Mesa ps3.476); OpenArena's opengl1 renderer and Yamagi Quake II 1-1.3% in
the earlier one. On the Cell PPE the scalar loop is a load, two compares and two branches per
index, in order.

With NV30_MINMAX_VMX=1 (off by default; PowerPC only, and only where the CPU has AltiVec) arrays
of 16- and 32-bit indices of 64 or more are scanned eight (four) at a time: GCC's vector types,
which it compiles to lvx, vminuh/vmaxuh (vminuw/vmaxuw) in these functions
(vbo_minmax_vmx.h, target("altivec")). The unaligned head and the tail stay scalar; below 64
indices the whole scan does, as taking the lanes apart at the end goes through memory (a
load-hit-store on the PPE). Primitive restart indices are masked out (0xffff for the minimum, 0
for the maximum); a 16-bit array has none when the restart index is above 0xffff, and an array
of nothing but restart indices gives ~0u and 0 as the scalar loop does.

Checked against the scalar loops (PS3 tree, mesa/tests/minmax-vmx-test.c: every start
alignment, counts 0 to 300, restart off and with six restart indices, random, narrow and
top-of-range values, 2.7 million cases): on the host with GCC's generic vectors, and built with
the ppc64 cross compiler for -mcpu=cell and run under qemu-ppc64 (AltiVec).

Forwarded: no

